'''Burawi''' (Burawi: ''Af-Burawi'') is a [[Burano-Ormaic languages|Burano-Ormaic]] language spoken by approximately 350 million people across [[Southwestern Kaftia]], [[Abyala]], and diaspora communities. It is the most widely spoken language on the Southwestern Kaftian peninsula and serves as the official language of government, education, and media in [[Burawa]]. In addition to its native speakers in [[Burawa]], [[Fayeha]], [[Kijura]], [[Galwa]], [[Wolonia]], and [[Kwalib]], Burawi functions as a significant second language across [[Kaftia]] and in the former colonial territories of [[Burawi Cusub]].

Linguistically, Burawi is classified within the [[Burano-Ormaic languages|Burano-Ormaic]] family, closely related to the neighbouring [[Orma languages]]. However, it is unique for its complex lexical stratification: an ancient [[Mesogean language|Mesogean]] layer from historical trade, and a modern political layer derived from [[Darasian]] (Fosian) and [[Volisanian language|Volisanian]] sources.

== Phonology ==
Burawi has 28 consonant phonemes and 5 vowel qualities with phonemic length distinctions. Its phonology is notable for retaining pharyngeal consonants characteristic of the [[Burano-Ormaic languages|Burano-Ormaic]] family while incorporating ejective consonants from the [[Orma languages|Orma]] branch and additional sounds from [[Mesogean language|Mesogean]] and [[Volisanian language|Volisanian]] contact.

