=== Phonotactics ===
The syllable structure is generally '''(C)V(C)'''. Native roots do not permit initial consonant clusters. However, loanwords from [[Mesogean]] often undergo epenthesis to break up prohibited clusters (e.g., ''statua'' → ''istatwa'').

=== Prosody ===
Burawi employs a grammatical tone system typical of the [[Burano-Ormaic languages|Burano-Ormaic]] family, distinguishing gender and case through high and low tones. In [[Mesogean]] loanwords, a stress-accent system is superimposed, typically stressing the penultimate syllable.

=== Diachronic changes ===

Between the 16th and 19th centuries, Burawi underwent a series of phonological changes collectively designated the '''Modern Shift''' (''Beddelka Casriga''). The shift was driven by the language's expanding function as a trade [[lingua franca]], colonial administrative medium, and common speech across the linguistically diverse territories of the [[Kingdom of Burawa|Burawi Empire]]. As non-native populations adopted Burawi for commerce and governance, the language underwent systematic simplification comparable to the evolution of [[Mesogean language|Classical Mesogean]] into the modern [[Volisanian language|Volisanian]] and [[Berona|Beron]] vernaculars.

The principal phonological changes are:

* '''Vowel shortening''': Long vowels in [[Mesogean language|Mesogean]]-origin words reduced to short vowels. Classical ''Rebuublik'' became Modern ''Rebublik''; Classical ''Viisreegnaad'' became ''Visregnad''. Native [[Burano-Ormaic languages|Burano-Ormaic]] long vowels were less affected.
* '''Syllable elision''': Unstressed syllables in polysyllabic loanwords dropped. ''Volisaniya'' reduced to ''Volsanya''; ''Dignitas'' to ''Dignas''.
* '''Final weakening''': Terminal consonants and Mesogean case endings eroded. The masculine ending ''-us'' reduced to a schwa trace or was lost entirely (''Liber-us'' → ''Libre''). The abstract suffix ''-itas'' weakened to ''-ita'' (''Dignitas'' → ''Dignita'').
* '''Article fusion''': The definite article suffixes (''-ka'', ''-ta'') merged orthographically with the noun stem. Classical ''Nin-ka'' became Modern ''Ninka''.

The Modern Shift affected Mesogean-origin vocabulary disproportionately. Native Burano-Ormaic roots, which lacked the redundant vowel length and inflectional suffixes of the Mesogean stratum, remained largely stable.

==== Morphological simplification ====

Classical Burawi distinguished three noun classes: native roots marked with determiners (''-ka/-ta''), old Mesogean loans retaining fossilised case endings (''-us/-a''), and modern loans from [[Darasian]] and [[Volisanian language|Volisanian]] treated as native. In the modern language, these three classes have '''merged into a single system'''. All nouns take native-style determiners regardless of etymological origin. The Mesogean case endings survive only as traces in formal and literary registers.

==== Syntactic shift ====

Classical Burawi maintained a strict '''SOV''' (Subject-Object-Verb) word order. Formal and written Modern Burawi retains this order. In colloquial speech, however, the language has drifted toward '''SVO''' under the sustained influence of [[Volisanian language|Volisanian]], a language with which Burawi has been in continuous contact through [[Mesogean Sea|Mesogean]] trade and colonial interaction. The SVO pattern is most pronounced in urban centres and among younger speakers.

== Dialects and varieties ==

=== Eastern and Western Burawi ===

The [[1709 Split|division of the Burawi state in 1709]] into the [[Kingdom of Burawa]] (east) and the [[Burawi Kingdom of Galwa]] (west) produced over a century of independent linguistic development. '''Eastern Burawi''', centred on the capital and the [[Mesogean Sea|Mesogean]] coast, underwent the Modern Shift more rapidly, driven by trade, colonial administration, and contact with [[Volisanian language|Volisanian]] and other [[Illypnia|Illypnian]] languages. '''Western Burawi''', centred on [[Galwa]] and the [[Great Kaftian Lake]] basin, developed separately, retaining more conservative phonology but innovating in vocabulary through contact with [[Sarafoni language|Sarafoni]]- and [[Dakari language|Dakari]]-speaking populations.

By the 19th century, the two varieties were mutually intelligible but exhibited consistent differences in vowel quality, intonation, and lexical preference — a degree of divergence comparable to that between [[Volisanian language|Volisanian]] and [[Berona|Beron]]. Following reunification, Eastern Burawi was adopted as the basis for the modern standard language. Western Burawi persists as a regional variety in [[Galwa]] and the lake region.

=== Cusub Burawi ===

The colonial territory of [[Burawi Cusub]] in eastern [[Abyala]] developed a distinct dialect over several centuries of separation from the metropolitan language. '''Cusub Burawi''' absorbed indigenous Abyalan vocabulary, underwent independent phonological innovations, and developed distinctive rhythmic and intonational patterns. The relationship between Cusub Burawi and metropolitan Burawi is comparable to that between the Abyalan and Terdolian varieties of [[Volisanian language|Volisanian]].

